# Cursor Rules — Finance & ITR App

These rules are not optional. They exist because this app handles financial
records and tax figures, where code that *looks* correct can be quietly
wrong. Read them before writing code.

---

## What this project is

A single-user, hosted web app that turns bank transactions into a categorized
P&L and a balance sheet, and prepares figures for filing an Indian ITR-3. It
tags transactions by entity (multiple businesses). Built with Next.js (App
Router), TypeScript, PostgreSQL, and Google Gemini 3.1 Flash Lite for AI assistance.

The full design lives in `/docs`. Read `/docs/architecture.md` first; each
flow has its own `/docs/flow-*.md`. The docs are the source of truth for how
the app is meant to work.

---

## Hard rules — never violate these

1. **AI proposes, code computes, the human confirms.** The AI model is used
   only to *read* messy input and *draft* suggestions (categories,
   descriptions, document extraction). Every number that lands in the books or
   on a tax return is calculated by plain deterministic code. A language model
   must never compute a P&L total, a depreciation figure, an EMI split, a tax
   number, or any financial amount.

2. **Money is never a floating-point number.** Store and compute money as
   integers in the smallest unit (paise) or as a fixed-precision decimal.
   Floats lose precision and corrupt totals.

3. **The EMI split comes from the loan schedule, in code.** The
   principal/interest split of a loan EMI is read from the matched
   `loan_schedule_row` and computed by code — never produced by AI, never
   derived from a hardcoded "standard EMI" amount. Match each transaction
   against each row's own `emi_amount` (schedules have irregular first/last
   instalments).

4. **Duplicate detection is the fingerprint + unique index.** Detect duplicate
   transactions via the `fingerprint` hash and the unique index on
   `(source_account_id, fingerprint)`. Never scan existing rows to compare,
   and never use a "is this date after the latest transaction" heuristic.

5. **Nothing counts until confirmed.** A transaction affects the P&L or
   balance sheet only when `status = confirmed`. AI output is always a
   proposal awaiting human confirmation.

6. **Server-side only for sensitive work.** Database access, the Gemini API
   key, and all computation (P&L, depreciation, EMI matching, tax) live in
   server-side code — route handlers, server actions, server components.
   Never in a client component. The API key is never in the frontend bundle.

7. **Write the audit log.** Every change to a transaction line (category,
   description, split) is recorded in the audit log. Do not add an edit path
   that bypasses it.

---

## Development sequence — build one phase at a time

The app is built in phases, in this order. **Do not start a phase until the
previous one is complete and tested.** Each phase must end in a state the user
can actually test — ideally against real data. If asked to jump ahead, point
out the dependency and confirm before proceeding.

- **Phase 0 — Skeleton.** Next.js project, PostgreSQL connected, the schema
  from `architecture.md` §3 as real migrations, login/auth.
- **Phase 1 — Reference data (Books nav).** Categories (main + sub tree on
  `/categories`), entities (side-nav selector + add flow; no Entities tab),
  Assets (accounts, bank profiles), Liabilities (loans). Learned rules UI is
  deferred until import/categorize (Phase 2–3).
- **Phase 2 — Import.** CSV/Excel parsing, the fingerprint/dedup, transactions
  landing as `pending_review`.
- **Phase 3 — Categorize.** The AI categorization pass and the Transactions
  page review/confirmation UI (`pending_review` filter).
- **Phase 4 — Computation.** The P&L and balance-sheet engines (pure
  functions); opening-balances setup. Test this phase hardest.
- **Phase 5 — Loans.** Loan setup, schedule extraction + checksum
  verification, EMI matching and splitting.
- **Phase 6 — Dashboard & Reports.** Built now, because real numbers exist to
  display.
- **Phase 7 — Tax prep.** The ITR figure preparation and dual computation.

Within a phase, the same rule applies: build a piece, test it, then proceed.
The computation functions (P&L, depreciation, EMI split, fingerprint) are the
most important things to test — verify them before building on them.

---

## Documentation discipline

The `/docs` flow documents describe how each flow is *meant* to work. They are
a record of intent, not just a mirror of the code. Keep them and the code in
agreement.

- **Before editing any flow, read its `/docs/flow-*.md` first.** That doc is
  what you compare your work against.
- **If a requested change — or code you are about to write — would diverge
  from what the flow doc describes, STOP and tell the user before writing it.**
  Name the doc and quote the part that conflicts. Let the user decide whether
  to keep the documented behavior or to proceed and update the doc.
- **If you read existing code that already contradicts its flow doc, surface
  it** rather than silently coding alongside the inconsistency.
- **Never edit a flow doc silently to match new code.** A doc changes only as
  a deliberate, user-confirmed step — and when it does, it changes in the
  same commit as the code, and its "last reviewed against code" line is
  updated.
- Code entry points for a flow should carry a one-line pointer comment to the
  relevant doc, e.g. `// Transaction import flow — see docs/flow-transaction-import.md`.

---

## Code conventions

- **TypeScript, strict.** No `any` on anything financial — transactions,
  amounts, categories, schedule rows, tax figures. Types are a guardrail here,
  not decoration.
- **The schema is shared and single-sourced.** Define data types once (the
  ORM schema) and use them across server and client. Do not redefine a
  transaction or category shape ad hoc.
- **Errors are surfaced, not swallowed.** Do not catch an error and continue
  silently. For a financial app a silent failure is worse than a visible one
  — a wrong number nobody noticed is the worst outcome.
- **Computation lives in pure, testable functions** separate from UI and from
  data fetching. The P&L engine, depreciation, EMI matching, and the
  fingerprint hash should each be a function you can call with inputs and
  assert on outputs.
- **Verify against the lender's checksums.** When extracting a loan schedule,
  always run the checksum verification (sums of the rows vs. the stored
  totals) before trusting the data.
- Keep formatting and naming clean and consistent — but the rules above
  matter more than style.
- **No page-level horizontal scroll.** Dashboard pages must not overflow the
  viewport width. Use `min-w-0` / `max-w-full` on flex and grid children;
  clip page overflow on the dashboard shell; put wide tables in a local
  `overflow-x-auto` container only. Never use negative horizontal margins that
  widen the page.
- **Dashboard layout:** outer shell is `h-dvh overflow-hidden`; side nav stays
  fixed height; only the main content column scrolls (`overflow-y-auto` on
  `main`), never the whole page including the nav.

---

## Ask the user first before

- **Changing the data model** — adding, removing, or renaming a column or
  table. The schema is the foundation; changes need a deliberate decision.
- **Adding a new dependency / library.** Propose it and explain why; do not
  pull one in unannounced.
- **Anything on the "explicit permission" line** that touches real money or
  external services — but in normal development this mostly means: confirm
  before running destructive database operations (dropping tables, deleting
  data, resetting migrations).
- **Diverging from a flow doc** — already covered above, restated here because
  it is the easiest rule to forget.
